# XSH sem_getvalue()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# For a locked semaphore the page allows zero or a negative number whose
# size is a count of waiters at some time during the call; no particular
# negative convention is required. EINVAL is a permission. The page lists
# no EOVERFLOW.

assertion  XSH.sem_getvalue.001
anchor     functions/sem_getvalue.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/sem_getvalue.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       sem_getvalue() of an unlocked semaphore returns 0 and stores the
           semaphore's value (1, 5, and {SEM_VALUE_MAX} where it fits an
           int), the same on a second call, and does not change the state
           (the small ones then still give that many trywait successes).
test       t/001-reports-value.c

assertion  XSH.sem_getvalue.002
anchor     functions/sem_getvalue.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       sem_getvalue() of a semaphore of value 0 with no thread waiting
           stores 0 (the "negative number" for no waiters is zero), both
           for a new one and after a post and a wait.
test       t/002-zero.c

assertion  XSH.sem_getvalue.003
anchor     functions/sem_getvalue.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       Single-threaded, the value stored follows the operations:
           5, 4 after a wait, 5 after a post, 0 after five trywaits,
           1 after another post.
test       t/003-tracks.c

assertion  XSH.sem_getvalue.004
anchor     functions/sem_getvalue.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       With two threads blocked in sem_wait() on the semaphore,
           sem_getvalue() returns 0 and stores 0, -1 or -2 (never a
           positive number or a larger count); the waiters are not
           disturbed (two posts then release both) and the value is 0
           afterwards.
note       -1 is accepted too: the count is of waiters at some time during
           the call, and a helper thread may not yet be blocked. No
           negative convention is required.
test       t/004-waiters.c
